# Serialization policy

## Two ratchets, one workflow

The wallet and the daemon chain store are both gated by
[`schema-snapshot.yml`](../../.github/workflows/schema-snapshot.yml), by
different mechanisms shaped to what each persists:

| Tree | What is snapshotted | Snapshot dir | Version constant | Regenerate |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `rust/shekyl-engine-state/**` | the `postcard_schema::Schema` of each persisted block | `rust/shekyl-engine-state/schemas/*.snap` | one per block (table below) | `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 cargo test -p shekyl-engine-state schema_snapshot` |
| `rust/shekyl-chain-store/**` | the fixture **encodings** of each `codec::Canonical` impl, the **table catalogue** (name, shape, key/value type per `schema.rs` definition), and the **property-cell catalogue** (key, scope, value-codec name per `property_cells!` row) | `rust/shekyl-chain-store/schemas/*.snap` | `SCHEMA_VERSION` in `src/codec/schema_version.rs`, for the whole store | `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 cargo test -p shekyl-chain-store codec::snapshot_tests` |

The chain store snapshots bytes rather than a type schema because its codecs
are hand-written fixed-width encodings, not serde derives, and because the
same bytes are the state digest's fold input
([`DAEMON_REDB_STORE.md`](../../docs/design/DAEMON_REDB_STORE.md) §11.1(b)):
a codec move is a consensus-oracle move, so the gate pins the output, not the
declaration. Two catalogues cover the rest of §11.1(b), where layout moves
without any codec's bytes moving: `schemas/tables.snap` (a table added,
removed or re-keyed — an old binary trips over it at `open_table`) and
`schemas/properties.snap` (a `properties` cell added, re-keyed, re-scoped
or re-typed — a new `ChainState` cell is digest-domain growth even when its
value codec already has a fixture). It has **one** version constant because
the store rebuilds rather than migrates (§11.1(a)) — any snapshot change
under `rust/shekyl-chain-store/schemas/` requires `SCHEMA_VERSION` to be
**numerically greater** at the PR head than at its base, with no per-codec
registry to keep in sync. Meta-tests in
[`snapshot_tests.rs`](../../rust/shekyl-chain-store/src/codec/snapshot_tests.rs)
hold the gate's own subject (rule 47): every `impl Canonical for` in the crate
has a snapshot and vice versa, every fixture encodes to its codec's
`FIXED_WIDTH`, every `TableDefinition` / `MultimapTableDefinition` in
`schema.rs` is inside the `tables!` invocation the table catalogue is emitted
from, every `key: "…"` in `property.rs` is a `PROPERTY_CELLS` row, the
assertion is armed unless `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS` is set (in which case exactly
one test fails loudly), and the workflow file still runs this module by name
and reads `SCHEMA_VERSION` with the declaration grammar the source uses.

**A codec snapshot's input is constructed inline, never a shared fixture.**
The snapshot's subject is the encoding. Build its input from a fixture some
other crate owns and the subject becomes the fixture: every legitimate change
to that fixture reads as a codec change, the paired-bump gate above demands a
`SCHEMA_VERSION` bump for a codec nobody touched, and someone under time
pressure supplies it — the version has now moved for a fixture edit, which
is the one thing the gate exists to make impossible. Observed 2026-09-25:
`codec::alt::test_block_bytes` embedded `shekyl_chain_rules::harness::
fixture::coinbase`; E6 slice 6 gave every harness coinbase its `tx_extra`
(CEN-I19/I20) and `schemas/alt_block.snap` moved with a codec whose layout
had not. The block is built inline now and the snapshot is byte-identical.
A snapshot fixture may *use* shared constants (a point, a hash) whose value
no rule will ever move; it may not use a shared *transaction or block*,
which rules move.

History: from `DRS-0` (2026-09-13) to `DRS-E1` increment 2 the chain-store
glob was attached to this rule with **no gate behind it**, and said so here.
That paragraph is gone because its subject is; the FOLLOWUPS row that carried
the gap closed with the gate.

Persisted blocks in Rust — today the six inside
`rust/shekyl-engine-state/` (`WalletLedger`, `LedgerBlock`,
`BookkeepingBlock`, `TxMetaBlock`, `SyncStateBlock`, `StakingBlock`), and any
future block type that lands on disk — carry an explicit **version constant**
whose sole job is to let a binary reject a file whose wire format it
cannot read. Changing the wire format without bumping the constant is
a silent data-corruption hazard: an older binary can accept a newer
file, decode fields into the wrong slots, and produce wrong balances,
wrong subaddresses, or wrong sync state with no error reported.

The mid-rewire hardening pass (commit 4, see
[`docs/MID_REWIRE_HARDENING.md`](../../docs/MID_REWIRE_HARDENING.md)
§3.4) converts this discipline from cultural invariant into mechanical
check via `postcard-schema` snapshots. This rule is the prose
counterpart: a reviewer looking at a PR diff can check it by eye, and
the CI enforces it on every landing.

## Funds-first tier placement

Before this rule's version-pairing even applies, you have to know a field
belongs in a persisted (tier-4) block at all. New wallet-tunable state is
placed by a **funds-first** decision order — the funds question is asked
*first*, before "is it user-tunable?":

1. **Does resetting this field to its default lose or endanger funds?** →
   **Tier 4: a sealed `shekyl-engine-state` block** — which this rule governs
   (strict version, snapshot-pinned, refuse-to-load). Stop here.
2. Network-fixed with no legitimate per-wallet variance? → Tier 1
   (`NetworkSafetyConstants`), plus a CLI-ephemeral override for sessions.
3. User-tunable and *not* funds-threatening? → Tier 3 (advisory
   `<base>.prefs.toml`).

All four tiers are specified in [`docs/WALLET_PREFS.md`](../../docs/WALLET_PREFS.md)
§2; tier 4 (sealed blocks) is the layer whose version-pairing this rule enforces.
The trap this order exists to prevent: a user-chosen, persistent,
funds-load-bearing field (`staking_enabled`, `p_slot`, the bonded set)
reflexively filed into advisory TOML, where a tamper-quarantine default-resets
it and endangers funds. Such fields are tier 4 — which is exactly why
`StakingBlock` carries a version constant in the table below.

## The pairing

Each persisted block owns exactly one version constant. A PR that
changes a block's **on-disk wire format** must also change that
constant's declaration line. The pairings are:

| Block                    | Version constant                  | Source file                                             |
|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| `WalletLedger`           | `WALLET_LEDGER_FORMAT_VERSION`    | `rust/shekyl-engine-state/src/wallet_ledger.rs`         |
| `LedgerBlock`            | `LEDGER_BLOCK_VERSION`            | `rust/shekyl-engine-state/src/ledger_block.rs`          |
| `BookkeepingBlock`       | `BOOKKEEPING_BLOCK_VERSION`       | `rust/shekyl-engine-state/src/bookkeeping_block.rs`     |
| `TxMetaBlock`            | `TX_META_BLOCK_VERSION`           | `rust/shekyl-engine-state/src/tx_meta_block.rs`         |
| `SyncStateBlock`         | `SYNC_STATE_BLOCK_VERSION`        | `rust/shekyl-engine-state/src/sync_state_block.rs`      |
| `StakingBlock`           | `STAKING_BLOCK_VERSION`           | `rust/shekyl-engine-state/src/staking_block.rs`         |

`WalletLedger` carries both the bundle-level `format_version` **and**
every inner block's `block_version`. A change that only affects one
inner block bumps that block's constant; a change that restructures
the bundle envelope bumps `WALLET_LEDGER_FORMAT_VERSION`; a change
that does both bumps both.

The chain store's pairing is one row, deliberately:

| Store | Version constant | Source file |
|---|---|---|
| every `codec::Canonical` impl in `shekyl-chain-store` | `SCHEMA_VERSION` | `rust/shekyl-chain-store/src/codec/schema_version.rs` |

`SCHEMA_VERSION` is sealed into a fresh store's first transaction and any
other value on reopen is a refusal, not a migration. Its declaration carries
a History list; the bump edits the list in the same commit (rule 91's
"a constant's doc is part of the edit"), and a meta-test checks the current
value has an entry.

## What counts as a wire-format change

Any of these, on any persisted block, requires a constant bump:

1. **Adding a field.**
2. **Removing a field.**
3. **Renaming a field** (including via `#[serde(rename = "…")]`).
4. **Reordering fields.** Postcard is positional at the wire; two
   fields of the same type can swap places on the disk without a Rust
   type error, which is exactly the failure mode this rule exists to
   catch.
5. **Changing a field's type** to one that produces different wire
   bytes. Examples: `u32` → `u64`, `[u8; 32]` → `Vec<u8>`, `Option<T>`
   → `T` (drops the `0x00 | 0x01` tag byte).
6. **Changing a `#[serde(with = "…")]` helper** to one that emits a
   different wire representation.

These are **not** wire-format changes and do not require a bump:

- Doc-comment edits.
- Adding or removing `#[derive(Debug)]`, `#[derive(Clone)]`, or any
  other non-serde derive.
- Refactoring the in-memory representation in a way that the serde
  impl compensates for (but if `#[serde(with = "…")]` changes as part
  of the refactor, see point 6 above).
- Adding a `#[serde(skip)]` annotation **only** if the field was never
  serialized in a released version. Adding `#[serde(skip)]` to a
  previously-serialized field **is** a wire-format change: older files
  still contain the bytes, and the new binary would misalign while
  reading them.

## Mechanical enforcement

Two tests + two CI jobs enforce this policy, landed in commits 4 and
5 of the hardening pass. The two halves cover complementary failure
modes: the schema snapshot catches wire-format drift, and the
zeroize-field grep catches in-memory secret-wipe drift that the
snapshot structurally cannot see (since `Zeroizing<[u8; 32]>` and
`[u8; 32]` produce byte-identical postcard output).

### Schema snapshot (commit 4 — §3.4)

1. `cargo test -p shekyl-engine-state schema_snapshot` — asserts the
   derived `postcard_schema::Schema` of every persisted block matches
   the committed `.snap` under
   `rust/shekyl-engine-state/schemas/`. Any drift — add, remove,
   rename, reorder, type change — fails with a unified diff naming
   the offending field.
2. `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 cargo test -p shekyl-engine-state schema_snapshot`
   — the intentional regeneration path. Run this after a deliberate
   schema change.
3. `.github/workflows/schema-snapshot.yml` — on every PR into `dev`,
   asserts (1) and additionally cross-checks the PR diff: if any
   `.snap` file changed, the paired source file must be in the diff
   **and** the `pub const <NAME>:` declaration line must appear on
   either side of that file's unified diff. PRs that change a `.snap`
   without bumping the paired constant fail the workflow with a
   GitHub annotation on the offending file.

### Chain-store codec snapshot (DRS-E1 increment 2)

The same workflow, the same shape, one tree over:

1. `cargo test -p shekyl-chain-store codec::snapshot_tests` — for each
   `codec::Canonical` impl, encodes a fixed fixture set and asserts
   the hex matches the committed `.snap` under
   `rust/shekyl-chain-store/schemas/`; also asserts each fixture
   decodes back to itself and encodes to exactly `FIXED_WIDTH` bytes.
   Renders `schema::catalogue()` against `tables.snap` and
   `PROPERTY_CELLS` against `properties.snap`. Drift fails with a
   unified diff and the rebuild-not-migrate instructions.
2. `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 cargo test -p shekyl-chain-store codec::snapshot_tests`
   — regenerates. The `codec_snapshot_assertions_are_armed` test fails
   under this variable on purpose, so a CI run with it set cannot pass.
3. The workflow's bump job: if any `.snap` under the chain-store
   `schemas/` directory is in the PR diff, the value of
   `SCHEMA_VERSION` parsed from `src/codec/schema_version.rs` at the
   PR head must be greater than the value parsed at the PR base (or
   the file must be born in the PR). A rewrite of the declaration line
   that leaves the number alone does not pass.

### Zeroizing-field grep (commit 5 — §3.5)

4. `scripts/ci/check_zeroize.sh` — walks `shekyl-engine-state/src/`
   (production code only; `#[cfg(test)]` modules and multi-line
   function-signature parameters are excluded via paren-depth
   tracking) and emits every `[u8; N]` or `Vec<u8>` field
   declaration. Each hit must either (a) carry a `Zeroizing<...>` /
   `SecretKey<...>` wrapper on the same line, or (b) appear verbatim
   in `rust/shekyl-engine-state/.zeroize-allowlist`.
5. `.github/workflows/zeroize-check.yml` — runs the script on PRs
   that touch the wallet-state source tree, the allowlist, the
   script, or the workflow. Missing entries (new unwrapped field)
   and stale entries (allowlisted field removed without also
   removing the line) both fail with pinpoint messages — the file
   cannot rot with ghost entries that would silently re-admit a
   future field of the same spelling.

The two checks together: commit 4 ensures the wire shape doesn't
change without a version bump; commit 5 ensures the in-memory
secret-wipe contract doesn't regress while the wire shape stays
stable. Either alone is insufficient; together they cover the two
distinct migration hazards.

## Procedure for an intentional schema change

1. Edit the Rust struct(s) in the normal way.
2. Run `cargo test -p shekyl-engine-state schema_snapshot` and
   observe the failure diff. Confirm the diff is what you meant.
3. Run `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 cargo test -p shekyl-engine-state schema_snapshot`
   to regenerate the `.snap` files for the affected blocks.
4. Bump the paired version constant(s) in the same commit. A one-step
   bump (`N` → `N+1`) is normally sufficient; larger jumps are
   reserved for coordinated multi-field migrations and must be
   justified in the commit message.
5. If the block is read back in by an older code path (e.g. a
   migration loader), update that path to handle the old version
   alongside the new one. This is the actual work; the snapshot just
   keeps you honest.
6. Commit. CI will re-run the snapshot assertion and the pairing
   check; both should pass.

## Why a mirror-struct pattern appears in two places

`TransferDetails` (in `src/transfer.rs`) and `TxSecretKey` (in
`src/tx_meta_block.rs`) have fields that use `#[serde(with = "…")]`
helpers the `postcard_schema::Schema` derive cannot introspect: the
derive reads the declared Rust type, not the serde-helper-rewritten
wire type. Both types implement `Schema` via a compile-only mirror
struct (`TransferDetailsSchema`, `TxSecretKeySchema`) whose fields use
wire-native types (`Vec<u8>` for length-prefixed byte sequences) —
wire-identical to the originals, but with a derivable schema. The
pattern is local to each file, reviewable at the call site, and keeps
the snapshot mechanism honest without coupling us to an upstream
`postcard-schema` release cadence.

If you add a new persisted field whose type is not natively
`Schema`-able (e.g. an FCMP++ proof type, a curve25519-dalek
`EdwardsPoint`, a ML-KEM ciphertext), extend the enclosing block's
mirror struct the same way — add a matching field of wire-native type
— rather than patching the derive.

## Escape hatch

None. There is no allowlist and no override. A persisted block that
does not carry a version constant does not exist in Shekyl
wallet-state; a wire-format change without a bump does not land.
Policy deviations require an amendment to
[`docs/MID_REWIRE_HARDENING.md`](../../docs/MID_REWIRE_HARDENING.md)
§3.4 landing as a prerequisite PR.

## Rationale

- Wallets live for years. A one-off wire change that slips past
  review will be read by a binary from three years ago running on
  someone's cold-storage laptop, and there is no recall.
- The cost of the check is ~70 lines of Rust (the snapshot module)
  plus a ~170-line workflow. The cost of a missed change is a user
  opening a wallet into corrupted balances with no error message.
- Mechanical checks outlive the humans who wrote them. "Remember to
  bump the constant" is a cultural invariant that erodes; "CI rejects
  your PR" is one that does not.

## Envelope version vs payload version — name them apart

A format has two version numbers with different jobs, and they must not
share a name or a field: the **envelope/armoring version** (the prefix a
parser dispatches on — never fed into any signed preimage) and the
**payload/layout version** (the canonical byte inside the signed or
persisted bytes — the security or schema boundary). SM-R-5 is the
precedent: `shekylmsgsig1.`'s `1` is envelope; `layout_version` is the
preimage's `sig_format_version`, one value appearing twice by
construction. A name that could mean either (`version`, `v`) is the bug
vector; qualify every version identifier with which of the two it is.
